# 🪪 A digital identity wallet, end to end, in Elixir

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:attesto, "~> 2.3"},
  {:attesto_client, "~> 2.7"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
  {:eqrcode, "~> 0.2"}
])
```

## What we're building

Three parties, one credential — the shape of the EU Digital Identity Wallet:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant I as 🏛️ Issuer
  participant W as 🪪 Wallet
  participant V as 🔎 Verifier
  I->>W: Issue a Person ID credential (SD-JWT VC), bound to the wallet's key
  V->>W: "Prove only what I ask" — fresh nonce + DCQL query
  W->>V: Present just those claims + a holder signature over the nonce
  V->>V: Check issuer signature · holder binding · nonce · disclosed claims
```

The issuer signs a credential once, bound to the wallet's key. The verifier asks
for *specific* claims. The wallet reveals only those — everything else never
leaves the device. That's **selective disclosure**.

Everything below runs locally on [`attesto`](https://hex.pm/packages/attesto)
(issuer/verifier core) and [`attesto_client`](https://hex.pm/packages/attesto_client)
(the wallet) — no HTTP servers or mock transports. The encrypted example
requires Attesto 2.3 and AttestoClient 2.7. See the
[Credential wallet guide](credential-wallet.md) to connect this local exercise
to real issuance, authorization, trust and consent flows.

## Keys

```elixir
# The issuer's signing key. Its public half is the trust anchor a verifier pins.
issuer_jwk = JOSE.JWK.generate_key({:ec, "P-256"})
issuer_pem = issuer_jwk |> JOSE.JWK.to_pem() |> elem(1)
{_kty, issuer_public} = JOSE.JWK.to_public_map(issuer_jwk)

# The wallet's holder key — never leaves the wallet. The credential is bound to it,
# and the wallet signs each presentation with it.
holder_jwk = JOSE.JWK.generate_key({:ec, "P-256"})
{_kty, holder_public} = JOSE.JWK.to_public_map(holder_jwk)

:keys_ready
```

## 1. 🏛️ The issuer mints a credential

A Person Identification Data (PID) credential, bound to the wallet's key:

```elixir
credential =
  Attesto.SdJwtVc.issue(
    [iss: "https://issuer.example.gov", vct: "urn:eudi:pid:1", pem: issuer_pem],
    claims: %{
      "given_name" => "Ada",
      "family_name" => "Lovelace",
      "birthdate" => "1815-12-10",
      "nationality" => "GB",
      "age_over_18" => true,
      "age_over_21" => true
    },
    cnf: %{"jwk" => holder_public}
  )

Kino.Text.new(credential)
```

That string is a **SD-JWT VC**: an issuer-signed JWT, then one `~`-separated
_disclosure_ per claim. Each disclosure can be revealed or withheld
independently — the signature covers all of them either way.

## 2. 🪪 The wallet receives and holds it

On receipt, the wallet verifies the issuer signature before trusting anything:

```elixir
{:ok, received} = Attesto.SdJwtVc.verify(credential, issuer_public)

held = %{
  format: "dc+sd-jwt",
  credential: credential,
  claims: received.claims,
  holder_binding: %{"jwk" => holder_public}
}

received.claims
|> Map.drop(["cnf", "iss"])
|> Enum.map(fn {k, v} -> %{"claim" => k, "value" => inspect(v)} end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "In the wallet")
```

In a real flow the wallet would pick this up from an
`openid-credential-offer://` link — the kind you'd scan to onboard a credential.
Here's that QR:

```elixir
offer =
  %{
    "credential_issuer" => "https://issuer.example.gov",
    "credential_configuration_ids" => ["eu.europa.ec.eudi.pid.1"],
    "grants" => %{
      "urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:pre-authorized_code" => %{
        "pre-authorized_code" => "demo-code-123"
      }
    }
  }
  |> JSON.encode!()

("openid-credential-offer://?credential_offer=" <> URI.encode(offer))
|> EQRCode.encode()
|> EQRCode.svg(width: 240)
|> Kino.HTML.new()
```

## 3. 🔎 The verifier asks — you choose what it may see

Tick the claims the verifier is allowed to learn, then hit **Present**. Watch
what reaches the verifier — and what stays locked in the wallet.

Try presenting only `age_over_21`: a bar confirms you're old enough and learns
**neither your name nor your birthdate**.

```elixir
request = %AttestoClient.Wallet.PresentationRequest{
  client_id: "https://bar.example",
  nonce: 16 |> :crypto.strong_rand_bytes() |> Base.url_encode64(padding: false),
  response_uri: "https://bar.example/present",
  response_mode: "direct_post",
  client_metadata: %{
    "vp_formats_supported" => %{
      "dc+sd-jwt" => %{"sd-jwt_alg_values" => ["ES256"], "kb-jwt_alg_values" => ["ES256"]}
    }
  },
  dcql_query: %{"credentials" => []}
}

pid_fields = ~w(given_name family_name birthdate nationality age_over_18 age_over_21)

form =
  Kino.Control.form(
    for(f <- pid_fields, do: {String.to_atom(f), Kino.Input.checkbox(f, default: f == "age_over_21")}),
    submit: "🪪 Present to verifier"
  )

frame = Kino.Frame.new()

Kino.listen(form, fn %{data: data} ->
  requested = for {field, true} <- data, do: Atom.to_string(field)

  out =
    if requested == [] do
      Kino.Markdown.new("_Tick at least one claim for the verifier to request._")
    else
      # The verifier's request names only the claims it needs (DCQL).
      req = %{
        request
        | dcql_query: %{
            "credentials" => [
              %{
                "id" => "pid",
                "format" => "dc+sd-jwt",
                "meta" => %{"vct_values" => ["urn:eudi:pid:1"]},
                "claims" => Enum.map(requested, &%{"path" => [&1]})
              }
            ]
          }
      }

      # The wallet builds a presentation: only the requested disclosures, plus a
      # Key Binding JWT signed over the verifier's nonce (so it can't be replayed).
      {:ok, vp_token} =
        AttestoClient.Wallet.Presentation.build_vp_token(%{"pid" => held}, req,
          holder_keys: %{"pid" => holder_jwk}
        )

      # The verifier verifies issuer signature, holder binding, and nonce.
      {:ok, results} =
        Attesto.VpToken.verify(vp_token,
          nonce: req.nonce,
          audience: req.client_id,
          expected_query_ids: ["pid"],
          issuer_jwks: issuer_public
        )

      # Take just the PID data fields (ignore JWT metadata like iat/iss/vct).
      [verified_pid] = results["pid"]
      seen = Map.take(verified_pid.claims, pid_fields)
      hidden = pid_fields -- Map.keys(seen)

      seen_rows =
        seen
        |> Enum.map(fn {k, v} -> "| `#{k}` | #{inspect(v)} |" end)
        |> Enum.join("\n")

      Kino.Markdown.new("""
      ### ✅ Presentation accepted
      Issuer signature valid · bound to the holder's key · nonce matched.

      **What the verifier learned**

      | claim | value |
      |---|---|
      #{seen_rows}

      **🔒 Never left the wallet:** #{if hidden == [], do: "_(nothing — all disclosed)_", else: Enum.map_join(hidden, ", ", &"`#{&1}`")}
      """)
    end

  Kino.Frame.render(frame, out)
end)

Kino.Layout.grid([form, frame], boxed: true, gap: 16)
```

## 4. 🔐 Encrypt the presentation for its recipient

Selective disclosure limits what the verifier receives. Encryption protects
the response on its way there. In `direct_post.jwt`, both the credential and
the correlation state go inside the encrypted response.

The following example plays both roles locally. In a real wallet, the verifier's
public encryption key comes from the authenticated request metadata; the wallet
never receives the verifier's private key. Always verify that request and obtain
consent before building the response.

```elixir
recipient_key = JOSE.JWK.generate_key({:ec, "P-256"})
{_type, recipient_public} = JOSE.JWK.to_public_map(recipient_key)
recipient_public = Map.merge(recipient_public, %{"alg" => "ECDH-ES", "kid" => "demo-recipient"})

encrypted_request = %{
  request
  | response_mode: "direct_post.jwt",
    state: "demo-correlation-state",
    client_metadata: %{
      "vp_formats_supported" => request.client_metadata["vp_formats_supported"],
      "jwks" => %{"keys" => [recipient_public]},
      "encrypted_response_enc_values_supported" => ["A256GCM", "A128GCM"]
    },
    dcql_query: %{
      "credentials" => [
        %{
          "id" => "pid",
          "format" => "dc+sd-jwt",
          "meta" => %{"vct_values" => ["urn:eudi:pid:1"]},
          "claims" => [%{"path" => ["age_over_21"]}]
        }
      ]
    }
}

{:ok, encrypted_vp_token} =
  AttestoClient.Wallet.Presentation.build_vp_token(%{"pid" => held}, encrypted_request,
    holder_keys: %{"pid" => holder_jwk}
  )

{:ok, encrypted_form} =
  AttestoClient.Wallet.Presentation.build_response(encrypted_request, encrypted_vp_token)

# This is the only form field that would be posted. It contains a compact JWE.
Map.keys(encrypted_form)
```

Now act as the verifier: decrypt with its private key, check the returned state,
and verify the presentation's issuer signature, holder binding and nonce.

```elixir
{:ok, decrypted_bytes, protected_header} =
  Attesto.JWE.decrypt(recipient_key, encrypted_form["response"])

decrypted_response = JSON.decode!(decrypted_bytes)
true = decrypted_response["state"] == encrypted_request.state

# Final OID4VP responses contain an array of presentations in both modes.
[received_presentation] = decrypted_response["vp_token"]["pid"]

{:ok, verified_response} =
  Attesto.VpToken.verify(%{"pid" => [received_presentation]},
    nonce: encrypted_request.nonce,
    audience: encrypted_request.client_id,
    expected_query_ids: ["pid"],
    issuer_jwks: issuer_public
  )

Kino.Layout.grid([
  Kino.Markdown.new("**Credential format:** `dc+sd-jwt` · **Encryption:** `#{protected_header["enc"]}`"),
  Kino.DataTable.new(
    for({claim, value} <- Map.take(hd(verified_response["pid"]).claims, pid_fields),
      do: %{"disclosed claim" => claim, "value" => inspect(value)}),
    name: "Decrypted and verified contents"
  )
])
```

The holder signature and nonce checks are still necessary after decryption.
Encryption authenticates the ciphertext to its recipient; it does not replace
credential issuer trust or the user's disclosure decision.

## What just happened

* The **issuer** signed the credential once, binding it to the wallet's key.
* The **verifier** sent a fresh nonce and a DCQL query naming only the claims it needed.
* The **wallet** returned a presentation with *only* those disclosures, plus a
  Key Binding JWT proving possession of the bound key — signed over the nonce,
  so a captured presentation can't be reused elsewhere.
* The **verifier** checked the issuer signature, the holder binding, and the
  nonce, and saw nothing beyond what it asked for.
* The encrypted example delivered the presentation and state as one JWE and
  verified the disclosed contents after decryption.

That privacy model — prove a fact without handing over the document — is the
whole point of the EU Digital Identity Wallet, and here it's a few dozen lines
of Elixir.

The same libraries also do ISO **mdoc** (mobile driving licence), DPoP
sender-constrained issuance, batch issuance, key/wallet attestation, and the
encrypted credential exchanges and bounded deferred polling. The full OID4VCI
issuer + OID4VP verifier HTTP surface is available via
[`attesto_phoenix`](https://hex.pm/packages/attesto_phoenix).

Continue with the [Credential wallet guide](credential-wallet.md) for real
offers, OAuth/PAR callbacks, HAIP certificates and attestation providers. The
existing issuer/verifier and OIDC RP certifications cover their listed roles;
wallet certification requires its own conformance run and submission.
